@page "/css-colours"

<PageTitle>CSS colours</PageTitle>
<h1>CSS colours</h1>
<p>The kit paints nothing itself — every border, message, and spinner you see reads a handful of CSS custom properties from the sample's stylesheet. Override those tokens on any ancestor and the whole validation surface recolours at once.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "Listing.cs", "CssColours.razor", "CssColours.razor.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li>Everything the kit colours flows through CSS custom properties in the SAMPLE's stylesheet — invalid borders, messages, the summary accent, even the pending spinner all read the same handful of tokens.</li>
            <li>Recolouring is overriding those tokens on any ancestor element — zero library involvement; the kit never sees a colour.</li>
            <li>That is a different seam from renaming: the <a href="/bootstrap">Bootstrap page</a> uses <code>FormidableCssOptions</code> to remap the state class NAMES onto another library's classes. Token override recolours; class-name remap re-targets whose stylesheet applies.</li>
            <li><strong>Title</strong> — required (error). <strong>Description</strong> — an exclamation mark draws a warning. <strong>Tags</strong> — more than five draws an info. Same validator as Severity levels.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Submit empty — then pick a new error colour and watch borders, messages, and the summary accent all follow.</li>
            <li>Focus a field and change the accent — ring and spinner colours follow.</li>
            <li>Type <code>!</code> in Description, blur, then recolour warning.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<div class="field"><label>Error <input type="color" @bind="Error" @bind:event="oninput" /></label></div>
<div class="field"><label>Accent <input type="color" @bind="Accent" @bind:event="oninput" /></label></div>
<div class="field"><label>Warning <input type="color" @bind="Warn" @bind:event="oninput" /></label></div>

@* "26" is 15% alpha appended to the accent hex for the ring token; it assumes a 6-digit
   hex, which is what <input type="color"> always produces. *@
<div style="@WrapperStyle">
    <FormidableForm Model="_listing" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid">
        <FormidableSummary />

        <div class="field"><label>Title <FormidableInputText For="() => _listing.Title" @bind-Value="_listing.Title" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _listing.Title" /></div>
        <div class="field"><label>Description <FormidableInputText For="() => _listing.Description" @bind-Value="_listing.Description" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _listing.Description" /></div>
        <div class="field"><label>Tags (comma-separated) <FormidableInputText For="() => _listing.Tags" @bind-Value="_listing.Tags" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _listing.Tags" /></div>

        <div class="actions"><button type="submit">Submit</button></div>
    </FormidableForm>
</div>

<p role="status">@_status</p>
